export const researchMarkdown = "# The Quranic Meaning of Z-K-W (زكاة) — Comprehensive Research Document\n\n**Prepared from:** verse-by-verse study of all **56 distinct Z-K-W root verses** (59 occurrences,\n20 word forms) in `quran-simple-min.txt` (Tanzil Simple-Minimal, 6236 verses), with\ndiacritics-stripped matching via `quran-clean.txt`.\n\n**Method:** Each verse studied in its Quranic context (surrounding verses + thematic parallels),\nwith grammar, lexicon, and cross-referencing of every parallel formula in the Quran. Look-alike\nforms were excluded after reading them: **زكريا / وزكريا** (Zechariah — root ز-ك-ر, 7 refs) and\n**يلمزك** (9:58 — \"finds fault with you\"; the ك is a pronoun suffix, not a radical). Study order:\nthematic clusters A→E (root anchors → the claim and the grant → the purifier sent → the founding\nformula → the institution), canonical order inside each cluster.\n\n**Files:** `1_Zakah_Verses.md` (collected verses), `2_Study_Order.md` (study plan, all 56 ticked),\n`3_Study_Notes.md` (verse-by-verse journal), `PROGRESS.md` (live thesis draft).\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — The settled core\n\nThe root Z-K-W (ز-ك-و) names **purity-with-growth**: the release of a portion that cleans what\nremains and causes it to increase. Not a tax, not a percentage, not a once-a-year receipt — a\ntransfer whose stated double effect is **cleansing and growth**.\n\nThe Quran supplies every term of the definition in its own words:\n\n1. **Purity is stated.** 9:103: `خُذْ مِنْ أَمْوَالِهِمْ صَدَقَةً تُطَهِّرُهُمْ وَتُزَكِّيهِم بِهَا` —\n   \"Take from their wealth a ṣadaqah by which you **cleanse** them and **grow-purify** them.\" The\n   two verbs share one object and one instrument. `ṭahāra` removes; `zakāh` increases.\n2. **Growth is stated.** 30:39 contrasts ribā — which \"does not grow with God\" — with zakāh, whose\n   givers are named **الْمُضْعِفُونَ** (*al-muḍʿifūn*, the multipliers). 2:276 corroborates: God\n   erases ribā and **makes the charities grow** (`yurbī al-ṣadaqāt`).\n3. **The instrument is stated.** 92:18: `الَّذِي يُؤْتِي مَالَهُ يَتَزَكَّىٰ` — \"who **gives his\n   wealth**, purifying himself.\" Giving wealth *is* self-purification. The verb's object is the\n   self; the verb's instrument is money.\n4. **The act is stated.** 23:4: `وَالَّذِينَ هُمْ لِلزَّكَاةِ فَاعِلُونَ` — \"those who are **doers**\n   of the zakāh.\" A tax is paid; a zakāh is *done*.\n5. **The agent is stated.** 4:49, 24:21, 53:32: no one purifies himself; God purifies whom He\n   wills. The human act is the receptor of a divine grant, never a self-salvation.\n\nThe root has **three stable poles**:\n\n| Pole | Meaning | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **STATE** | purity-growth as a quality of a self, soul, child, or food | 18:74 · 19:19 · 19:13 · 18:81 · 18:19 · 91:9 |\n| **ACT** | the giving that produces it — done, given, sent ahead | 9:103 · 92:18 · 23:4 · 30:39 · 2:110 |\n| **GRANT** | God and His Messenger as the agents who actually purify | 4:49 · 24:21 · 2:129 · 3:164 · 62:2 |\n\nThe **STATE** pole is what the word *is*. The **ACT** pole is how it is *done*. The **GRANT** pole\nis *who makes it work*. The same stem, three directions.\n\nThe root also has a **law of increase** (Part 9) and a **breach-pattern** (Part 11): in every\nwithholding-context, the withholder has first *sold* something — revelation for a small price\n(2:174, 3:77), the Hereafter for the present (41:7), or his own verdict of purity (4:49, 53:32).\nThe anti-zakāh is always a **sale**, and zakāh is always a **release**.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 — Cluster A: The Root Anchors (17 verses)\n\nThe root's intrinsic meaning, read before any institution is attached: purity as a quality of a\nliving self, a child, food, a gaze, a boundary.\n\n**A1 (18:74)** — \"أَقَتَلْتَ نَفْسًا زَكِيَّةً بِغَيْرِ نَفْسٍ\" — a **pure soul** killed without a\nmatching soul. Purity is guiltlessness of a life. The root's first appearance is a person, not a\npurse.\n\n**A2 (19:19)** — \"غُلَامًا زَكِيًّا\" — a **pure boy**, announced to Maryam. Purity is given before\nthe person acts. Surah 19's purity-thread: Yahya receives *zakāh* (19:13), Isa is announced *zakī*\n(19:19), Maryam is purified (3:42).\n\n**A3 (19:13)** — \"وَحَنَانًا مِّن لَّدُنَّا وَزَكَاةً\" — **a zakāh** given to Yahya beside tenderness.\nHere the noun is an **inner endowment**, not money. The institutional zakāh is one visible form of\nthis endowment, not a different word.\n\n**A4 (18:81)** — \"خَيْرًا مِّنْهُ زَكَاةً\" — better **in zakāh**. The word is a **measurable grade**:\none child can be better in zakāh than another. Purity can increase.\n\n**A5 (18:19)** — \"أَيُّهَا أَزْكَىٰ طَعَامًا\" — which food is **purest**. The root measures quality:\nclean, wholesome, unspoiled. The contrast is corruption entering a thing.\n\n**A6 (2:232)** — \"ذَٰلِكُمْ أَزْكَىٰ لَكُمْ وَأَطْهَرُ\" — **more purifying and cleaner**. The\nQur'an's own gloss-pair. `ṭahāra` removes defilement; `zakāh` adds soundness. The pair recurs at\n9:103 and 33:33.\n\n**A7 (24:28)** — \"هُوَ أَزْكَىٰ لَكُمْ\" — returning from a house that did not admit you is **purer**.\nA boundary kept is purity.\n\n**A8 (24:30)** — \"ذَٰلِكَ أَزْكَىٰ لَهُمْ\" — the lowered gaze and guarded chastity are **purer**. The\nfirst wealth to be purified is the eye and the member.\n\n**A9 (91:9)** — \"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن زَكَّاهَا\" — **flourished who purifies it** (the soul). THE\nCENTRE. The object of tazkiyah is the self; the root's success-word is bound to it; its opposite is\n\"buried it\" (91:10).\n\n**A10 (79:18)** — \"هَل لَّكَ إِلَىٰ أَن تَزَكَّىٰ\" — \"will you purify yourself?\" addressed to\n**Pharaoh**. The invitation is universal, not sectarian.\n\n**A11 (87:14)** — \"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن تَزَكَّىٰ ۞ وَذَكَرَ اسْمَ رَبِّهِ فَصَلَّىٰ\" — purified, then\nmentioned the Name, then **stood**. THE BRIDGE TO Ṣ-L-W: the two roots meet in one verse-pair.\n\n**A12 (20:76)** — \"وَذَٰلِكَ جَزَاءُ مَن تَزَكَّىٰ\" — **the reward of the one who purified**. Tazkiyah\nis the entry-condition to Eden.\n\n**A13 (35:18)** — \"وَمَن تَزَكَّىٰ فَإِنَّمَا يَتَزَكَّىٰ لِنَفْسِهِ\" — purification is **only for\noneself**. Non-transferable, self-benefiting. The same verse pairs it with establishing the standing.\n\n**A14 (92:18)** — \"الَّذِي يُؤْتِي مَالَهُ يَتَزَكَّىٰ\" — **gives his wealth, purifying himself**. THE\nEQUATION: the verb's object is the self, its instrument is wealth. The sequel names the intention:\n\"only seeking the face of his Lord Most High\" (92:20).\n\n**A15 (80:3)** — \"لَعَلَّهُ يَزَّكَّىٰ\" — perhaps the **blind man** would purify. Purity is not\npurchased by rank.\n\n**A16 (80:7)** — \"وَمَا عَلَيْكَ أَلَّا يَزَّكَّىٰ\" — purification **cannot be coerced**. The\nMessenger conveys; the person acts.\n\n**A17 (53:32)** — \"فَلَا تُزَكُّوا أَنفُسَكُمْ\" — **do not declare yourselves pure**. The verdict is\nforbidden, not the act. The anti-self-certification rule.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 — Cluster B: The Claim and the Grant (4 verses)\n\nWho may purify, and who is refused.\n\n**B1 (4:49)** — \"يُزَكُّونَ أَنفُسَهُم ۚ بَلِ اللَّهُ يُزَكِّي مَن يَشَاءُ\" — **claim vs grant**. The\nsame verb, two subjects: human self-purification is a boast; divine purification is the reality.\n\n**B2 (24:21)** — \"مَا زَكَىٰ مِنكُم مِّنْ أَحَدٍ أَبَدًا وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ يُزَكِّي مَن يَشَاءُ\" — even\nthe root's simplest verb, *to be pure*, is impossible without God's favour and mercy. THE GRANT.\nThis verse forbids reading zakāh as self-salvation.\n\n**B3 (2:174)** — \"وَلَا يُزَكِّيهِمْ\" — the concealers of the Book, who sell it for a small price,\nare **not purified**. Withholding purification is the punishment. Note the parallel to 4:10:\nconcealing revelation is eating fire.\n\n**B4 (3:77)** — \"وَلَا يُزَكِّيهِمْ\" — the four-fold negation: no share, no speech, no look, no\npurification, for those who **sell** God's covenant for a small price. The anti-zakāh is a sale;\nzakāh is a release.\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 — Cluster C: The Purifier Sent (4 verses)\n\nThe Messenger as the agent of tazkiyah.\n\n**C1 (2:129)** — Abraham's prayer: recite, teach the Book, teach wisdom, **purify**. Tazkiyah is the\ngoal of revelation and the Messenger's commission — a formation, not a collection.\n\n**C2 (2:151)** — \"يُزَكِّيكُمْ\" — purification is mediated by a human **from among you**. Nearness is\npart of the mechanism.\n\n**C3 (3:164)** — \"مَنَّ اللَّهُ\" — purification is a **favour**, not a wage. Order: recite, purify,\nteach — one is purified in order to learn.\n\n**C4 (62:2)** — \"وَإِن كَانُوا مِن قَبْلُ لَفِي ضَلَالٍ مُّبِينٍ\" — tazkiyah is an **exodus from clear\nerror**. The same transfer-structure as Ṣ-L-W's \"out of darkness into light\" (33:43).\n\n---\n\n## Part 5 — Cluster D: The Founding Formula (14 verses)\n\nCovenant, prophets, and the ṣalāh-zakāh pair.\n\n**D1 (98:5)** — THE MINIMAL RELIGION: sincere worship + uprightness + establish the standing + give\nthe zakāh = \"the religion of the upright.\" Zakāh is one of four constitutive acts, not an optional\nextra.\n\n**D2 (2:43)** — the pair commanded to a whole community, with a shared posture: \"bow with those who\nbow.\"\n\n**D3 (2:83)** — zakāh inside a social-ethical covenant; the breach is `tawallā` — the same\nturning-away as the Ṣ-L-W fire-verses.\n\n**D4 (5:12)** — condition #2 of a five-condition covenant; the fifth is \"lend God a good loan.\" The\neconomic axis runs through the covenant, and the return is transferred to the hereafter.\n\n**D5 (2:110)** — zakāh is **sent ahead**; whatever good you send forward you find with God. An\ninvestment, not a loss.\n\n**D6 (2:177)** — zakāh appears **after** the list of spontaneous givings. Two layers: the open hand\nof love, then the standing obligation of the community. The giver gave \"despite love of it.\"\n\n**D7 (2:277)** — zakāh is the **anti-ribā act**: no fear, no grief, immediately after \"God makes the\ncharities grow\" (2:276).\n\n**D8 (7:156)** — zakāh is a condition for **mercy being written**. The giver is inside the circle.\n\n**D9 (19:31)** — Jesus: enjoined on me the standing and the zakāh **as long as I live**, tied to\n`barakah` — blessedness, increase. No state, treasury, or quota in the verse.\n\n**D10 (19:55)** — Ishmael commanded his **household** the pair. The family is the first unit of the\ninstitution.\n\n**D11 (21:73)** — \"We **inspired** them … the giving of the zakāh.\" The giving is revelation-shaped\nand a mark of leadership. Leaders are defined by what they give, not collect.\n\n**D12 (22:78)** — zakāh belongs to the **eased religion**: no constriction. Establish, give, hold\nfast.\n\n**D13 (4:162)** — the givers are a distinct class alongside the **firmly-rooted in knowledge**. Zakāh\nis an act of the knowing.\n\n**D14 (5:55)** — zakāh is the **mark of God's party**; the giver is an ally of God. \"While bowing\"\nfuses the two acts.\n\n---\n\n## Part 6 — Cluster E: The Institution (17 verses)\n\nCommunity, power, economy, and growth.\n\n**E1 (9:5)** — the **peace-condition**: repent, establish, give → release their way.\n\n**E2 (9:11)** — the **brotherhood-condition**: the same three → brothers in religion. Withholding\nkeeps one outside the bond.\n\n**E3 (9:18)** — a condition for **maintaining God's houses**: believe, establish, give, fear only\nGod.\n\n**E4 (9:71)** — the **economic nerve** of the believing community, men and women alike.\n\n**E5 (9:103)** — THE MECHANISM: take **from** their wealth a ṣadaqah that cleanses and grows them.\nOne act, two effects; the partitive `min` is decisive.\n\n**E6 (22:41)** — the **first act of the empowered**: establish, give, enjoin, forbid. The test of\npower is whether it gives.\n\n**E7 (23:4)** — THE ACT: \"**doers** of the zakāh.\" A tax is paid; a zakāh is done. It sits among\ncharacter-items, not line-items.\n\n**E8 (24:37)** — the **economic test**: neither trade nor sale distracts from remembrance,\nestablishing, and giving.\n\n**E9 (24:56)** — a condition for **mercy**, paired with obedience to the Messenger.\n\n**E10 (27:3)** — the act of the **Hereafter-certain**. The one sure of the return gives now.\n\n**E11 (30:39)** — THE PIVOT: ribā does not grow with God; zakāh does, and its givers are\n**al-muḍʿifūn, the multipliers**. The Qur'an moves the increase-verb from the lender to the giver.\n\n**E12 (31:4)** — word-for-word identical to 27:3: the formula is **stable across suras**, therefore\nan institution, not a local instruction.\n\n**E13 (33:33)** — the household command, ending in **ṭahāra**: remove impurity, purify. Zakāh and\npurification are one movement.\n\n**E14 (41:7)** — THE NEGATIVE DEFINITION: withholding zakāh is coordinated with **disbelief in the\nHereafter**. The giver believes in the return; the withholder denies it.\n\n**E15 (58:13)** — zakāh follows **forgiveness**. The failed giver is re-commanded; grace precedes\nthe act.\n\n**E16 (73:20)** — the **eased arrangement**: commanded in the same breath as the concession. \"Lend\nGod a good loan\" makes the giver a creditor of God.\n\n**E17 (4:77)** — the **peace-act and courage-training**: giving loosens the grip on wealth before\nlife is asked for.\n\n---\n\n## Part 7 — The Noun-Lexicon of Z-K-W\n\n| Lexeme | Form | Meaning | Where |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ZAKĀH | noun (f.) | purity-with-growth; the institutional giving that produces it | 2:43, 2:83, 2:110, 98:5, etc. (26×) |\n| ZAKĀH | indefinite noun | a purity-endowment; a grade of purity | 19:13 · 18:81 |\n| ZAKĀH | indefinite noun | any act of zakāh-giving | 30:39 |\n| ZAKĪ | adjective | pure, guiltless, sound | 18:74 · 19:19 |\n| AZKĀ | elative | purer, more wholesome, better | 18:19 · 2:232 · 24:28 · 24:30 |\n| MUZAKKĪ / TAZKIYAH | Form II verb / verbal noun | the act and process of purifying-growing | 2:129, 3:164, 9:103, 91:9 |\n| MUTAZAKKĪ | Form V participle | the self-purifier | 92:18 · 35:18 · 87:14 |\n\nThe same noun serves the **quality** (19:13, 18:81), the **act** (30:39), and the **institution**\n(2:43 and the pair-formula). The word did not begin as a tax and acquire a spiritual gloss; it began\nas a quality of a sound self and became the name of the act that produces that quality.\n\n---\n\n## Part 8 — The Verb-Voice Table\n\n| Voice | Form | Where | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Simple state | زَكَا | 24:21 | to be pure (negated for humans without grace) |\n| Form II active | يُزَكِّي | 4:49 · 24:21 · 2:129 · 3:164 · 9:103 | to purify-grow (God, Messenger) |\n| Form II active (human claim) | يُزَكُّونَ | 4:49 | to declare oneself pure |\n| Form II imperative/negated | تُزَكُّوا | 53:32 | do not declare yourselves pure |\n| Form II perfect | زَكَّاهَا | 91:9 | he purified it (the soul) |\n| Form V | تَزَكَّىٰ / يَتَزَكَّىٰ | 87:14 · 20:76 · 35:18 · 92:18 · 79:18 | to purify oneself / grow in purity |\n| Form V assimilated | يَزَّكَّىٰ | 80:3 · 80:7 | to purify oneself |\n| Elative | أَزْكَىٰ | 18:19 · 2:232 · 24:28 · 24:30 | purer, more wholesome |\n| Adjective | زَكِيّ · زَكِيَّة | 19:19 · 18:74 | pure |\n| Noun | زَكَاة | 26× + 18:81 · 19:13 · 30:39 | purity-with-growth / the giving |\n\nThe voice-coverage is complete: **state** (zakā), **act** (zakkā, tazakkā), **grade** (azkā),\n**quality** (zakī), **institution** (zakāh). Every voice names the same field: purity that grows.\n\n---\n\n## Part 9 — The Law of Increase\n\n| Verse | What increases | What does not |\n|---|---|---|\n| 30:39 | zakāh given seeking God's face → **al-muḍʿifūn** (the multipliers) | ribā given to increase in people's wealth → **does not grow with God** |\n| 2:276 | God **makes the charities grow** (`yurbī al-ṣadaqāt`) | God **erases ribā** (`yamḥaqu`) |\n| 2:277 | the givers: reward, **no fear, no grief** | the accumulator: anxiety |\n| 92:18-21 | the giver who seeks God's face: **will be satisfied** | the one who gives to be repaid: no reward |\n| 9:103 | the taken ṣadaqah **cleanses and grows** | — |\n| 73:20 | what you send ahead you **find with God**, better and greater | what is kept is not sent |\n\nThe Qur'an's economic theology is a single transfer: **the increase-verb is moved from the lender to\nthe giver.** Ribā is the attempt to make money grow by charging; zakāh is the release by which God\nmakes it grow. The root's name for the second is *zakāh* — growth.\n\n---\n\n## Part 10 — The Two-Effect Vocabulary (ṭahāra + zakāh)\n\n| Verse | Cleansing | Growth |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9:103 | `tuṭahhiruhum` — cleanses them | `tuzakkīhim` — grows-purifies them |\n| 2:232 | `aṭhar` — cleaner | `azkā` — more purifying |\n| 33:33 | `yuṭahhirakum` — purify you thoroughly / remove the impurity | the zakāh commanded in the same verse |\n| 2:174 | — | `lā yuzakkīhim` — no purification (the negative) |\n| 24:21 | — | `mā zakā … abadan` — never pure (the negative) |\n\n`Ṭahāra` is subtraction: remove what defiles. `Zakāh` is addition: cause what remains to grow. The\nQur'an consistently puts them in that order. A reading that makes zakāh only \"charity\" loses the\nsecond half; a reading that makes it only \"growth\" loses the first.\n\n---\n\n## Part 11 — The Breach-Pattern (Sale → Loss)\n\n| Verse | What was sold | What is lost |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2:174 | concealed the Book, sold it for a small price | God will not purify them; fire in the belly |\n| 3:77 | sold God's covenant and oaths for a small price | no share, no speech, no look, no purification |\n| 41:7 | withheld the zakāh | reckoned a disbeliever in the Hereafter |\n| 4:49 | claimed self-purity | the claim is corrected by the divine grant |\n| 53:32 | declared the self pure | the verdict belongs to God alone |\n| 24:21 | followed Satan's steps | no one would ever be pure without grace |\n| 2:83 | turned away from the covenant | the giving abandoned with the standing |\n\nThe anti-zakāh is always a **sale**: something that should be released is converted into a price.\nThe withholder does not merely keep money; he sells a covenant, a revelation, or a verdict. Zakāh is\nthe reverse: it releases the portion without a price.\n\n---\n\n## Part 12 — The Wealth-Table (what is released, to whom)\n\n| Verse | The released thing | The recipients / shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2:177 | wealth, despite love of it | relatives, orphans, the poor, the wayfarer, askers, slaves |\n| 9:103 | **from** their wealth, a ṣadaqah | taken by the Messenger; cleanses and grows |\n| 92:18 | his wealth | given not to repay a favour, seeking God's face |\n| 30:39 | any zakāh | given seeking God's face; multiplied |\n| 2:3 / 8:3 | what God provided | spent (the open pair with the standing) |\n| 2:110 / 73:20 | whatever good is sent ahead | found with God |\n| 5:12 | a good loan to God | repaid with covering and gardens |\n| 2:274-276 | charity, secret and public | God makes it grow |\n\nThe object is always **a portion**, never the whole; the direction is always **outward**, never\nupward to a collector for its own sake; the return is always **with God**, never in the amount given.\n\n---\n\n## Part 13 — The Self-Table (the nafs as object)\n\n| Verse | The self-word | The act |\n|---|---|---|\n| 91:9-10 | `al-nafs` (91:7) | purified / buried |\n| 53:32 | `anfusakum` | do not declare pure |\n| 4:49 | `anfusahum` | they declare pure; God purifies |\n| 35:18 | `nafsihi` | purifies only for himself |\n| 92:18 | implied self | gives wealth, purifying himself |\n| 24:21 | `minkum` | not one of you would be pure |\n| 18:74 | `nafsan zakiyyah` | a pure soul |\n| 79:18 | `tazakkā` | will you purify yourself? |\n\nThe root's constant object is the **self**. Money is the instrument, never the subject. This is the\nsingle strongest argument against reading zakāh as a revenue tax: the grammar's object is always a\nperson, and the wealth is always the means.\n\n---\n\n## Part 14 — The Time-Table\n\n| Time-form | Where | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lifelong | 19:31 (`mā dumtu ḥayyā`) | the command runs as long as one lives |\n| Habitual / household | 19:55 (`kāna yaʾmuru ahlahu`) | transmitted daily in the family |\n| Standing practice | 27:3 · 31:4 (`yuʾtūna`) | a participle, not an event |\n| Sent ahead | 2:110 · 73:20 (`tuqaddimū`) | value moved into the future |\n| Before the Day | 2:254 · 14:31 (parallel) | giving belongs to this side |\n| After forgiveness | 58:13 | grace precedes the act |\n| In ease and constraint | 73:20 · 22:78 | calibrated to capacity |\n\nZakāh is a **standing practice** (participle) with a **lifelong horizon** and a **daily household\nform**. It is not a annual event in the Qur'an's grammar; the annual cycle is a later juristic\nspecification, and no percentage is given in the text.\n\n---\n\n## Part 15 — The Covenant-Table\n\n| Verse | The covenant | Zakāh's place |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2:83 | Israel: one God + social excellence + speech + standing + giving | inside the list; breach = turning away |\n| 5:12 | Israel: five conditions | condition #2, with the good loan as #5 |\n| 7:156 | mercy written for the guarding, giving, believing | condition of mercy |\n| 98:5 | the upright religion | one of four constitutive acts |\n| 22:78 | the milla of Abraham, no constriction | establish, give, hold fast |\n| 9:11 | the brotherhood covenant | the third condition |\n| 9:5 | the peace covenant | the third condition |\n\nZakāh is never a standalone rule. It is always a **clause in a covenant** — with God, with the\ncommunity, or with the household. To withhold it is to break a relationship, not to fail a\ntransaction.\n\n---\n\n## Part 16 — The Face-Table (the intention)\n\n| Verse | The intention-clause | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 30:39 | `تُرِيدُونَ وَجْهَ اللَّهِ` | seeking God's face — the condition of increase |\n| 92:20 | `إِلَّا ابْتِغَاءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ الْأَعْلَىٰ` | only seeking the face of his Lord Most High |\n| 2:177 | `عَلَىٰ حُبِّهِ` | despite love of it — the inner cost |\n| 9:103 | taken by the Messenger, for their purification | the act has a minister, not a market |\n| 2:110 / 73:20 | `لِأَنفُسِكُم` | for yourselves — the self-benefit |\n| 4:49 / 53:32 | the self as claimant | the wrong intention: self-certification |\n\nThe intention decides whether the release is zakāh or a sale. The same coin can be ribā, a price, or\na zakāh; the difference is the face sought.\n\n---\n\n## Part 17 — The Bridge Verses\n\n**Surah 87 (the hinge to Ṣ-L-W):** 87:14 \"flourished who purified\" → 87:15 \"and mentioned the Name\nof his Lord and stood.\" `tazakkā` → `dhakara` → `fa-ṣallā`. The two roots of this project meet in\none verse-pair: purification prepares the standing; the standing carries the purified. And 87:18-19\nsays this was already written in the scrolls of Abraham and Musa.\n\n**Surah 92 (the bridge to the noun):** 92:5-7 the giver, guarder, truster → 92:18 \"who gives his\nwealth, purifying himself\" → 92:19-21 \"not to repay a favour, only seeking the face of his Lord\nMost High; and he will be satisfied.\" The verb `tazakkā` is defined by the act of giving wealth.\nThis is the exact point where the quality becomes the institution.\n\n**Surah 9 (the mechanism):** 9:103 \"take **from** their wealth a ṣadaqah that cleanses and grows\nthem\" → 9:99 spending counted as the Messenger's ṣalawāt → 9:84 the withheld ṣalāh on the dead. The\ngiving, the standing, and the honor-economy are one system.\n\n**Surah 30 (the pivot):** 30:39 ribā does not grow; zakāh multiplies. The same sura opens with the\nRomans' defeat and closes with the economics of the face. Increase belongs to God, not to the\nlender.\n\n**Surah 2 (the two layers):** 2:177 the spontaneous givings, then the zakāh; 2:275-277 ribā erased,\ncharity grown, the givers fearless. The chapter's economic argument runs from the open hand to the\nstanding obligation to the promise of no grief.\n\n---\n\n## Part 18 — The Settled Meaning\n\nThe root Z-K-W (ز-ك-و) names **purity-with-growth**: the release of a portion that cleans what\nremains and causes it to increase — a sustained practice, not a percentage, not a receipt.\n\n**The SAME stem, THREE directions:**\n\n- **STATE — what the word is.** A quality of a sound self: a pure soul (18:74), a pure boy (19:19),\n  a purity-endowment (19:13), a grade of betterment (18:81), wholesome food (18:19), a kept boundary\n  (24:28), a guarded gaze (24:30). The root's success-word belongs here: \"flourished who purifies\n  it\" (91:9).\n\n- **ACT — how it is done.** The release of a portion of wealth: taken from their wealth (9:103),\n  given despite love of it (2:177), given not to be repaid (92:18), given seeking the Face (30:39),\n  done as a work (23:4), sent ahead (2:110), lent to God (5:12, 73:20). The act has two effects —\n  cleansing and growth — and one intention — the Face.\n\n- **GRANT — who makes it work.** God purifies whom He wills (4:49, 24:21); the Messenger is sent to\n  purify (2:129, 2:151, 3:164, 62:2); no one purifies himself (24:21); no one may declare himself\n  pure (53:32). The human act is the receptor of a divine grant, never a self-salvation.\n\n**The noun zakāh** is the institutional name of the ACT pole, willingly kept and publicly\nregulated — one of the four constitutive acts of the upright religion (98:5), a clause in every\ncovenant, the mark of God's party (5:55), the first act of the empowered (22:41), the economic nerve\nof the community (9:71), and the condition of brotherhood (9:11) and mercy (24:56).\n\n**The verb zakkā** names the act and its effect: to purify and to cause to grow. **The elative\nazkā** names the grade: one thing can be purer than another. **The adjective zakī** names the\nquality: sound, guiltless, clean.\n\n**The law of increase** is the root's own economics: ribā claims increase and has none with God;\nzakāh is the release God multiplies, and its givers are named *the multipliers* (30:39). The Qur'an\nmoves the increase-verb from the lender to the giver.\n\nThe word is therefore neither \"alms\" nor \"tax\" nor \"purification\" alone. It is **the growth that\ncomes by release** — the portion let go so that the self, the wealth, and the community may be\nclean and increase. The Quran builds its covenant, its community, its economy, and its hope of Eden\non this single motion: **give the portion, and grow.**\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Qur'an uses the root Z-K-W (ز-ك-و) to name **purity-with-growth** — the release of a portion\nthat cleanses what remains and causes it to increase. The same stem names:\n\n- **The pure self** (STATE pole): the sound soul, the pure child, the wholesome food, the kept\n  boundary, the guarded gaze — a quality that can be graded and given.\n- **The giving** (ACT pole): the portion taken from wealth, given despite love of it, given without\n  return, given seeking the Face — a work with doers, sent ahead, lent to God.\n- **The Purifier** (GRANT pole): God who purifies whom He wills, and the Messenger sent to recite,\n  teach, and purify — so that no one claims the verdict for himself.\n\nThe root's own law is the law of increase: what is released grows; what is grasped does not. Ribā\nis the attempt to grow by holding; zakāh is the growth that comes by letting go. The Quran's word\nfor the second is the word for purity itself.\n\nThe zakāh is both what you **are** (a self being made sound) and what you **do** (a portion released\ninto the hands of God). Its measure is not a percentage but a direction: **outward, toward the Face,\nfor the growth of what remains.**\n";

export const conclusionDetailedMarkdown = "# Conclusion — The Quranic Meaning of Z-K-W (زكاة)\n\n**Source:** Verse-by-verse study of all **56 distinct Z-K-W root verses** (59 occurrences, 20 word\nforms) in the Quran, documented in `3_Study_Notes.md` (56 entries) and synthesized in\n`Final_Research_Zakah.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Root's Core Meaning\n\nThe root Z-K-W (ز-ك-و) names **purity-with-growth**: the release of a portion that cleanses what\nremains and causes it to increase. Not a percentage, not a receipt, not a once-a-year transaction.\n\nThe Quran states each term of the definition:\n\n1. **Purity** — 9:103: \"take from their wealth a ṣadaqah by which you **cleanse** them and\n   **grow-purify** them.\"\n2. **Growth** — 30:39: ribā \"does not grow with God,\" while the givers of zakāh are named\n   **الْمُضْعِفُونَ** (*al-muḍʿifūn*, the multipliers). 2:276: God \"makes the charities grow.\"\n3. **The instrument** — 92:18: \"who **gives his wealth**, purifying himself.\"\n4. **The act** — 23:4: \"**doers** of the zakāh.\" A tax is paid; a zakāh is done.\n5. **The agent** — 4:49, 24:21, 53:32: no one purifies himself; God purifies whom He wills.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Three Poles\n\n| Pole | Meaning | Lexeme | Where |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **STATE** | purity-growth as a quality of a self, soul, child, or food | زَكِيّ · زَكِيَّة · أَزْكَىٰ · زَكَاة | 18:74 · 19:19 · 19:13 · 18:81 · 18:19 · 91:9 |\n| **ACT** | the giving that produces it — done, given, sent ahead | الزَّكَاة · إِيتَاء · فَاعِلُونَ | 9:103 · 92:18 · 23:4 · 30:39 · 2:110 |\n| **GRANT** | God and His Messenger as the agents who actually purify | يُزَكِّي · يُزَكِّيهِمْ | 4:49 · 24:21 · 2:129 · 3:164 · 62:2 |\n\nThe **STATE** pole is what the word *is*. The **ACT** pole is how it is *done*. The **GRANT** pole\nis *who makes it work*. The same stem, three directions.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Two Effects — Cleanse and Grow\n\nThe Quran's own gloss-pair is **ṭahāra + zakāh**:\n\n| Verse | Cleansing | Growth |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9:103 | `tuṭahhiruhum` — cleanses them | `tuzakkīhim` — grows-purifies them |\n| 2:232 | `aṭhar` — cleaner | `azkā` — more purifying |\n| 33:33 | `yuṭahhirakum` — purify you thoroughly; remove the impurity | the zakāh commanded in the same verse |\n\n`Ṭahāra` is subtraction: remove what defiles. `Zakāh` is addition: cause what remains to grow. The\norder is always the same — cleanse, then grow. A reading that makes zakāh only \"charity\" loses the\ngrowth; a reading that makes it only \"growth\" loses the cleansing.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Law of Increase (Ribā vs Zakāh)\n\n| Verse | What grows | What does not |\n|---|---|---|\n| 30:39 | zakāh given seeking God's face → **the multipliers** | ribā given to increase in people's wealth → **does not grow with God** |\n| 2:276 | God **makes the charities grow** | God **erases ribā** |\n| 2:277 | the givers: reward, **no fear, no grief** | the accumulator: anxiety |\n| 92:18-21 | the giver seeking God's face: **will be satisfied** | the giver seeking repayment: no reward |\n| 73:20 | what you send ahead you **find with God** | what is kept is not sent |\n\nThe Quran moves the increase-verb from the lender to the giver. Ribā is the attempt to grow by\nholding; zakāh is the growth that comes by letting go. This is not a gloss imported from a lexicon:\n30:39 says it in the root's own letters.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The Act, Not a Tax\n\nThe strongest internal proof is **23:4**: `وَالَّذِينَ هُمْ لِلزَّكَاةِ فَاعِلُونَ` — \"those who are\n**doers** of the zakāh.\" Elsewhere the verb is `ātā` (give); here the Quran calls zakāh a **work with\ndoers**. It sits in a character-list — humility, guarding, chastity, the zakāh, trusts, covenants,\nthe standing — not in a fiscal list.\n\nThe second proof is **92:18**: giving wealth *is* self-purification. The verb's object is the self;\nits instrument is money. Money is never the subject of the root.\n\nThe third proof is the absence of any rate. The Quran names the act, its effect, its intention, and\nits recipients. It gives no percentage. The percentage is a later juristic specification, not a\nQuranic datum.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The Grant — Who Actually Purifies\n\n| Verse | Claim | Correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4:49 | they purify themselves | \"rather, God purifies whom He wills\" |\n| 24:21 | — | \"not one of you would ever be pure without God's favour and mercy\" |\n| 53:32 | — | \"do not declare yourselves pure; He knows best who is guarded\" |\n| 2:174 · 3:77 | they sell revelation for a small price | \"He will not purify them\" |\n| 2:129 · 3:164 · 62:2 | — | the Messenger is sent to **purify** |\n\nThe human act is the receptor of a divine grant, never a self-salvation. This is why the Quran can\ncommand the giving and still deny that the giver purifies himself: the command is the channel, not\nthe cause.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The Breach-Pattern (Sale → Loss)\n\n| Verse | What was sold | What is lost |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2:174 | concealed the Book, sold it for a small price | no purification; fire in the belly |\n| 3:77 | sold God's covenant and oaths for a small price | no share, no speech, no look, no purification |\n| 41:7 | withheld the zakāh | reckoned a disbeliever in the Hereafter |\n| 4:49 · 53:32 | claimed self-purity | the verdict is corrected |\n| 24:21 | followed Satan's steps | purity becomes impossible without grace |\n| 2:83 | turned away from the covenant | the giving abandoned with the standing |\n\nThe anti-zakāh is always a **sale**: something that should be released is converted into a price.\nThe withholder does not merely keep money; he sells a covenant, a revelation, or a verdict.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Why It Is Always Paired With the Standing\n\nZakāh appears in the ṣalāh-pair in 26 of its 59 occurrences. The pairing is not a listing habit; it\nis a structure:\n\n| Verse | The pair's work |\n|---|---|\n| 98:5 | the four acts of the upright religion |\n| 2:43 · 2:83 · 5:12 | the Israelite covenant |\n| 9:5 · 9:11 | peace and brotherhood conditions |\n| 9:18 | maintaining God's houses |\n| 22:41 | the first act of the empowered |\n| 24:37 | the non-distracted public life |\n| 31:4 · 27:3 | the Hereafter-certain |\n| 33:33 | the purified household |\n| 58:13 | after forgiveness |\n| 73:20 | the eased arrangement |\n\nThe standing is the vertical act; the zakāh is the horizontal act. The Quran will not let either\nstand alone: a standing without giving is incomplete, and a giving without standing is unanchored.\nAnd 87:14-15 puts them in sequence: **purify → mention the Name → stand.**\n\n---\n\n## 9. The Lexicon\n\n| Lexeme | Form | Meaning | Where |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ZAKĀH | noun (f.) | purity-with-growth; the giving that produces it | 2:43, 2:83, 98:5, etc. (26×) |\n| ZAKĀH | indefinite | a purity-endowment; a grade of purity | 19:13 · 18:81 |\n| ZAKĀH | indefinite | any act of zakāh-giving | 30:39 |\n| ZAKĪ | adjective | pure, guiltless, sound | 18:74 · 19:19 |\n| AZKĀ | elative | purer, more wholesome, better | 18:19 · 2:232 · 24:28 · 24:30 |\n| ZAKKĀ | Form II verb | to purify and cause to grow | 2:129 · 3:164 · 9:103 · 91:9 |\n| TAZAKKĀ | Form V verb | to purify oneself, grow in purity | 87:14 · 20:76 · 35:18 · 92:18 |\n\nThe same noun serves the quality (19:13), the act (30:39), and the institution (2:43). The word did\nnot begin as a tax and acquire a spiritual gloss; it began as a quality of a sound self and became\nthe name of the act that produces that quality.\n\n---\n\n## 10. The Bridge to Ṣ-L-W\n\n**87:14-15:** \"Flourished who purified, and mentioned the Name of his Lord, and stood.\"\n`tazakkā` → `dhakara` → `fa-ṣallā`. The two roots of this project meet in one verse-pair:\npurification prepares the standing; the standing carries the purified.\n\n**9:103:** the taken ṣadaqah cleanses and grows them, and the Prophet's standing is tranquility for\nthem. The giving and the standing are one system.\n\n**92:18-21:** the giver seeks the face of his Lord Most High — the same `wajh Allāh` as 30:39.\n\n---\n\n## 11. What Zakāh Is Not\n\n- **Not merely alms.** Alms are voluntary; zakāh is a covenant clause with a defined act and a\n  defined claim on the community (9:11, 9:5, 98:5).\n- **Not merely a tax.** A tax is paid; zakāh is *done* (23:4). A tax is collected for a treasury;\n  zakāh is given seeking the Face (30:39) and taken for purification (9:103).\n- **Not a percentage.** The Quran gives no rate. It gives an intention, a direction, and an effect.\n- **Not self-purification.** The claim is forbidden (53:32); the grant is God's (4:49, 24:21).\n- **Not a substitute for the standing.** The pair is stable across the whole Quran; neither replaces\n  the other.\n\n---\n\n## 12. The Settled Meaning\n\nThe root Z-K-W (ز-ك-و) names **purity-with-growth** — the release of a portion that cleanses what\nremains and causes it to increase.\n\n- **The STATE pole:** the sound self, the pure child, the wholesome food, the kept boundary, the\n  guarded gaze — a quality that can be graded and given.\n- **The ACT pole:** the portion taken from wealth, given despite love of it, given without return,\n  given seeking the Face — a work with doers, sent ahead, lent to God.\n- **The GRANT pole:** God who purifies whom He wills, and the Messenger sent to recite, teach, and\n  purify — so that no one claims the verdict for himself.\n\nThe root's own law is the law of increase: what is released grows; what is grasped does not. Ribā\nis the attempt to grow by holding; zakāh is the growth that comes by letting go. The Quran's word for\nthe second is the word for purity itself.\n\nThe zakāh is both what you **are** (a self being made sound) and what you **do** (a portion released\ninto the hands of God). Its measure is not a percentage but a direction: **outward, toward the Face,\nfor the growth of what remains.**\n\n---\n\n*First-pass study complete: 56/56 verses, 56 journal entries, 0 unchecked items. Synthesis: this\ndocument.*\n";

export const conclusionSimpleMarkdown = "# Zakah — In Plain Language\n\nA short account of what the study found, without the technical apparatus.\n\n---\n\n## The word in one sentence\n\n**Zakah is the growth that comes by release:** you give away a portion of what you have, and that\ngiving both cleans what remains and makes it increase.\n\n---\n\n## What the Quran actually says\n\n### 1. It names two effects, not one\n\n> \"Take from their wealth a charity by which you **cleanse** them and **grow-purify** them.\" (9:103)\n\nMost people hear only the first half — giving as charity, giving as duty. The verse names a second\neffect: growth. The Arabic uses two verbs for one act. Cleansing is subtraction; zakāh is addition.\n\n### 2. The Quran says what grows\n\n> \"Whatever you give as ribā so that it may increase in people's wealth — it does not increase with\n> God. And whatever you give of zakāh, seeking God's face — those are the **multipliers**.\" (30:39)\n\nThis is the key verse. The Quran takes the word \"increase\" away from interest and gives it to zakāh.\nInterest claims to make money grow; the Quran says it does not grow with God. Zakāh is the giving God\nmultiplies. The people who do it are literally called \"the multipliers.\"\n\n### 3. Giving wealth is how you purify yourself\n\n> \"Who gives his wealth, purifying himself.\" (92:18)\n\nThis is the definition. The verb is about the self; the instrument is money. The Quran does not say\n\"pray to become pure\" here — it says *give*, and you are purified.\n\n### 4. It is something you DO, not something you pay\n\n> \"Those who are **doers** of the zakāh.\" (23:4)\n\nEverywhere else the Quran says \"give the zakāh.\" Here it calls zakāh a work with doers. A tax is\npaid; a zakāh is done. And the Quran never gives a percentage. It gives a direction.\n\n### 5. You cannot purify yourself\n\n> \"Do not declare yourselves pure.\" (53:32)\n> \"Not one of you would ever be pure without God's favour and mercy.\" (24:21)\n\nThis is the safeguard. The act is yours, but the result is God's. Zakāh is not a spiritual\nachievement you can boast about. It is a channel for a gift.\n\n---\n\n## The three sides of the word\n\n| Side | What it means | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A quality** | a sound, pure, growing self | a pure soul (18:74), a pure child (19:19), wholesome food (18:19) |\n| **An act** | giving a portion of wealth | take from their wealth (9:103), give despite love of it (2:177) |\n| **A gift** | God and the Messenger make it work | God purifies whom He wills (4:49); the Messenger purifies (62:2) |\n\nThe same word covers all three. That is why \"charity\" is too small a translation: charity is only\nthe middle column.\n\n---\n\n## Why it is always next to the prayer\n\nZakāh appears with the prayer in most of its verses. The Quran treats them as two halves of one\nlife:\n\n- The prayer is the vertical act — turning toward God.\n- The zakāh is the horizontal act — turning toward people with what you have.\n\nAnd one verse puts them in order:\n\n> \"Flourished who purified, and mentioned the Name of his Lord, and stood.\" (87:14-15)\n\nPurify, remember, stand. The giving prepares the standing.\n\n---\n\n## What withholding it means\n\nThe Quran is unusually sharp here:\n\n> \"Those who do not give the zakāh, and they are disbelievers in the Hereafter.\" (41:7)\n\nWithholding is not treated as stinginess. It is treated as unbelief in the return — the belief that\nnothing comes back, so nothing should be let go. The person who gives believes there is a return.\nThe person who withholds has decided there is not.\n\nAnd the contrast is always a **sale**:\n\n- They sold the Book for a small price — so God will not purify them (2:174, 3:77).\n- They kept what should have been released — so they are counted deniers of the Hereafter (41:7).\n\nZakāh is the opposite of a sale. It is a release with no price.\n\n---\n\n## What this means in practice\n\n1. **It is a direction, not a percentage.** The Quran commands the release and names its intention;\n   it does not fix a rate. A rate can be a useful discipline, but it is not the meaning.\n2. **The intention is the face of God.** The same coin can be interest, a price, or a zakāh. The\n   difference is what you are seeking (30:39, 92:20).\n3. **It is for the growth of what remains.** You are not losing the portion; you are removing what\n   would have corrupted the rest.\n4. **It belongs to the community.** It is a condition of brotherhood (9:11), of peace (9:5), of\n   maintaining God's houses (9:18), and of the first act of anyone given power (22:41).\n5. **It is done, not paid.** Do it as a work — planned, habitual, quiet, and without a claim.\n\n---\n\n## The sentence to remember\n\n**Give the portion, and grow.**\n\nThat is the root: purity by release, increase by letting go.\n";
